From 1d4dac09a7c731a0a11a310ee1076330b4ddbe55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <stew675@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 04:29:52 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 13/35] WIP: default F32 dense weights off, and always-QSA
 prefill (drop the dense shortcut)

Two defaults, both measured, plus the revert of a failed experiment.

1) GGML_CUDA_MMB_F32SPLIT: 2 -> 0.  The F32 dense weights are all tiny-M (MoE
   router M=512, ssm alpha/beta M=48, hc *_inject M=4, the shared-expert gate
   M=1).  Both paths cost ~1.0 s at pp8192: the 128x128 f16-hi/lo tile computes
   a padded 128-row A tile (up to 128x wasted WMMA for M=4) and rocBLAS lands on
   the same shape bound.  IQ4_XS pp4096 896.0 -> 915.9, pp8192 896.8 -> 902.9.
   A 16x256 small-M tile was tried and is WORSE (870/847 vs 895/885 t/s - BN=256
   halves the block count), so it is reverted.  A dedicated tiny-M kernel is the
   real fix.

2) LLAMA_QSA_DENSE_SHORTCUT: default ON -> OFF, i.e. always QSA (maintainer
   decision).  The shortcut existed because below the selection width the top-k
   selects every cell, so sparse saved no attention work while paying the
   indexer.  qsa3 changed that: in the fully-dense pp2048 regime the qsa3
   attention kernel is 137.8 ms vs 149.9 ms for dense flash_attn_ext_f16 (8 %
   faster), and the path only still lost because indexer+top-k (20.9 ms) cost
   more than the 12.1 ms the kernel saved.  Net ~1 % at pp2048/4096 and ~0 at
   pp512/8192.  Removing it deletes the n_kv==width numerics seam and makes qsa3
   exercised at every context length (a -c 2048 exercise used to be silently
   dense).  Decode is unaffected - it stays dense via qsa_dense_decode_until.

PPL moves the right way: c16384 bf16 3.3900 -> 3.3821, q8_0 3.3879 -> 3.3861;
c32768 bf16 4.3353 -> 4.3397 (noise).  tg64 shallow 25.80 -> 25.83 (unchanged).
Greedy text coherent.
